# React & Next.js Patterns

## Component Guidelines

### Server vs Client Components

**Default to Server Components** unless you need:

- Event listeners (onClick, onChange, etc.)
- Browser APIs (window, localStorage, etc.)
- State (useState, useReducer, etc.)
- Effects (useEffect, useLayoutEffect)
- Client-side-only libraries

**Mark client components** with `'use client'` directive at the top:

```typescript
'use client';

import { useState } from 'react';

export function InteractiveComponent() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);
  // ...
}
```

### Component Props

Always define props with TypeScript interfaces:

```typescript
interface UserCardProps {
  user: User;
  onEdit?: (userId: string) => void;
  className?: string;
}

export function UserCard({ user, onEdit, className }: UserCardProps) {
  // ...
}
```

### Performance Optimization

Use `useMemo` for expensive computations:

```typescript
const sortedUsers = useMemo(() => users.sort((a, b) => a.name.localeCompare(b.name)), [users]);
```

Use `useCallback` for callback functions passed as props:

```typescript
const handleSubmit = useCallback(
  (data: FormData) => {
    mutation.mutate(data);
  },
  [mutation]
);
```

Use `React.memo()` strategically for expensive renders:

```typescript
export const ExpensiveComponent = React.memo(function ExpensiveComponent({ data }: Props) {
  // ...
});
```

## TanStack Query Patterns

### Query Client Configuration

**Smart Retry Logic:**

The QueryProvider should use intelligent retry that doesn't retry on 4xx errors (client errors):

```typescript
// In QueryProvider
defaultOptions: {
  queries: {
    retry: (failureCount, error) => {
      // Don't retry on 4xx errors (client errors)
      if (error instanceof Error && 'status' in error) {
        const status = (error as any).status;
        if (status >= 400 && status < 500) {
          return false; // Don't retry client errors
        }
      }
      return failureCount < 3; // Retry up to 3 times for server errors
    },
    retryDelay: (attemptIndex) => Math.min(1000 * 2 ** attemptIndex, 30000), // Exponential backoff
  },
  mutations: {
    retry: false, // Never retry mutations by default
  },
}
```

### Query Keys Factory Pattern

Always use a factory pattern for query keys:

```typescript
// lib/constants/query-keys.ts
export const userKeys = {
  all: ['users'] as const,
  lists: () => [...userKeys.all, 'list'] as const,
  list: (filters: UserFilters) => [...userKeys.lists(), filters] as const,
  details: () => [...userKeys.all, 'detail'] as const,
  detail: (id: string) => [...userKeys.details(), id] as const,
};
```

### Query Hooks

Create custom hooks for all queries:

```typescript
export function useUsers(
  filters: UserFilters = {},
  options?: Omit<UseQueryOptions<PaginatedResponse<User>>, 'queryKey' | 'queryFn'>
) {
  return useQuery<PaginatedResponse<User>>({
    queryKey: queryKeys.users.list(filters),
    queryFn: async () => {
      const { data } = await apiClient.get(API_ENDPOINTS.USERS.LIST, {
        params: filters,
      });
      return data;
    },
    staleTime: 5 * 60 * 1000, // 5 minutes
    gcTime: 10 * 60 * 1000, // 10 minutes
    placeholderData: (previousData) => previousData, // Keep previous data during refetch (smoother UX)
    ...options,
  });
}
```

**Key Options:**

- `placeholderData: (previousData) => previousData` - Shows previous data while refetching (avoids loading states)
- `retry: false` - Don't retry for auth-sensitive queries
- `retry: (failureCount, error) => { ... }` - Smart retry (don't retry 4xx errors)

### Mutation Hooks with Optimistic Updates

Implement optimistic updates for better UX:

```typescript
export function useUpdateUser() {
  const queryClient = useQueryClient();

  return useMutation({
    mutationFn: async ({ id, payload }: UpdateUserVariables) => {
      const { data } = await apiClient.put(API_ENDPOINTS.USERS.UPDATE(id), payload);
      return data;
    },
    onMutate: async ({ id, payload }) => {
      // Cancel outgoing queries
      await queryClient.cancelQueries({ queryKey: queryKeys.users.detail(id) });

      // Snapshot previous value
      const previousUser = queryClient.getQueryData<User>(queryKeys.users.detail(id));

      // Optimistically update
      if (previousUser) {
        queryClient.setQueryData<User>(queryKeys.users.detail(id), {
          ...previousUser,
          ...payload,
        });
      }

      return { previousUser };
    },
    onError: (error, { id }, context) => {
      // Rollback on error
      if (context?.previousUser) {
        queryClient.setQueryData(queryKeys.users.detail(id), context.previousUser);
      }
      toast.error('Failed to update user', {
        description: error.message,
      });
    },
    onSuccess: (data, { id }) => {
      queryClient.setQueryData(queryKeys.users.detail(id), data);
      toast.success('User updated successfully');
    },
    onSettled: (_, __, { id }) => {
      // Always invalidate after mutation
      queryClient.invalidateQueries({ queryKey: queryKeys.users.detail(id) });
      queryClient.invalidateQueries({ queryKey: queryKeys.users.lists() });
    },
  });
}
```

## Form Handling

### React Hook Form + Zod

Always use React Hook Form with Zod resolver:

```typescript
import { useForm } from 'react-hook-form';
import { zodResolver } from '@hookform/resolvers/zod';
import { loginSchema, LoginFormData } from '@/lib/schemas';

export function LoginForm() {
  const {
    register,
    handleSubmit,
    formState: { errors },
  } = useForm<LoginFormData>({
    resolver: zodResolver(loginSchema),
    defaultValues: {
      email: '',
      password: '',
    },
  });

  function onSubmit(data: LoginFormData) {
    mutation.mutate(data);
  }

  return (
    <form onSubmit={handleSubmit(onSubmit)}>
      {/* form fields */}
    </form>
  );
}
```

## Next.js Specific

### Data Fetching

- Use TanStack Query hooks in client components
- Use async/await in Server Components when needed
- Never fetch on client what can be fetched on server

### Image Optimization

Always use Next.js Image component:

```typescript
import Image from 'next/image';

<Image
  src="/avatar.jpg"
  alt="User avatar"
  width={48}
  height={48}
  priority // for above-the-fold images
/>
```

### Link Navigation

Always use Next.js Link for internal navigation:

```typescript
import Link from 'next/link';

<Link href={ROUTES.DASHBOARD.USERS.LIST}>
  View Users
</Link>
```

## Error Handling

### Component Error Boundaries

Every major feature should have error boundary:

```typescript
import { ErrorBoundary } from '@/components/shared';

<ErrorBoundary fallback={<ErrorFallback />}>
  <YourComponent />
</ErrorBoundary>
```

### Loading States

**Always use Skeleton loaders instead of LoadingSpinner whenever possible.**

Skeletons provide better UX by showing content structure while loading:

```typescript
import { Skeleton } from '@/components/ui/skeleton';

// ✅ Good - Use skeleton to show structure
{isLoading ? (
  <div className="space-y-4">
    {Array.from({ length: 3 }).map((_, i) => (
      <Card key={i}>
        <CardHeader>
          <Skeleton className="h-6 w-48" />
          <Skeleton className="h-4 w-32 mt-2" />
        </CardHeader>
        <CardContent>
          <Skeleton className="h-4 w-full" />
          <Skeleton className="h-4 w-3/4 mt-2" />
        </CardContent>
      </Card>
    ))}
  </div>
) : (
  <DataDisplay data={data} />
)}

// ❌ Avoid - Generic spinner doesn't show structure
{isLoading && <LoadingSpinner />}
```

**Use LoadingSpinner only when:**

- Skeleton structure would be too complex
- Loading is very brief (< 500ms)
- Inline button loading states
- Modal/dialog loading overlays

### Page Structure with Error Handling

Always structure pages with proper error handling:

```typescript
export default function UsersPage() {
  const [search, setSearch] = useState('');
  const debouncedSearch = useDebounce(search, 300);

  const { data, isLoading, isError, error } = useUsers({
    search: debouncedSearch || undefined,
    page: 1,
    limit: 10,
  });

  return (
    <div className="space-y-6">
      {/* Page Header - Always visible */}
      <PageHeader
        title="Users"
        description="Manage users and their permissions"
        actions={<Button>Add User</Button>}
      />

      {/* Search/Filter Section - Always visible */}
      <div className="flex items-center gap-4">
        <Input
          placeholder="Search users..."
          value={search}
          onChange={(e) => setSearch(e.target.value)}
        />
      </div>

      {/* Content Section - Conditional rendering */}
      {isLoading && (
        <UsersListSkeleton /> // ✅ Use skeleton
      )}

      {isError && (
        <Card className="p-6">
          <EmptyState
            title="Error loading users"
            description={error?.message || 'An unexpected error occurred'}
            action={
              <Button onClick={() => refetch()}>
                Try Again
              </Button>
            }
          />
        </Card>
      )}

      {!isLoading && !isError && isEmpty(data?.data) && (
        <Card className="p-6">
          <EmptyState
            title="No users found"
            description="Get started by creating your first user"
            action={<Button>Add User</Button>}
          />
        </Card>
      )}

      {!isLoading && !isError && !isEmpty(data?.data) && (
        <UsersList data={data.data} />
      )}
    </div>
  );
}
```

**Page Structure Pattern:**

1. **Page Header** - Always visible (title, description, actions)
2. **Filters/Search** - Always visible (user can interact while loading)
3. **Content** - Conditional based on state:
   - Loading → Skeleton (shows structure)
   - Error → Error state with retry option
   - Empty → Empty state with action
   - Success → Data display

This structure ensures:

- ✅ No layout shift (header/filters always visible)
- ✅ Clear visual feedback (skeleton shows what's coming)
- ✅ Better UX (can interact with filters while loading)
- ✅ Proper error handling with retry options
